#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168154 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find the *maximum* value of the quadratic form \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})=2x_1^2 + 4x_2^2,\n$$\n\n \n\nsubject to the constraint $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1.$ This type of problem is known as a **constrained optimization problem**.   \n\nOur constraint means we only consider vectors that lie on the unit sphere centered at the origin. \n\nSince our quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ has no mixed term $x_1x_2$, we can find its maximum value for $\\| \\mathbf{x} || = 1$ using algebraic manipulation, as follows.\n\nFirst, notice that $x_1^2 \\geq 0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n2x_1^2 \\leq 4x_1^2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & \\le 4{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =4({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1$ is equivalent to $x_1^2 + x_2^2 =1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2})=4(1)=4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x}) \\leq 4.\n$$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ can be no greater than $4.$ But before we conclude that $4$ is the maximum, we need to make sure that $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ can actually attain the value $4.$ Can we find a vector $\\mathbf x,$ with  $\\| \\mathbf{x} || = 1,$ such that $Q(\\mathbf{x}) = 4?$\n\nIndeed, we can. Setting $[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =2(0)^{2}+4(1)^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1,$  the maximum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ equals $4.$\n\nProceeding similarly, we can show that the minimum of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is equal to $2.$ \n\nGraphically, the situation can be visualized if we intersect the surface $z= {\\color{black}2x_1^2+4x_2^2}$ and the cylinder $\\color{black}x_1^2+x_2^2=1,$ as shown below:  \n\n![](images/39/39bfc05c9dd0733c1f4f867d21d1a5f018aacbf0ed91036a6f0981d701ba69d8.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "bf2f75a6-c5dc-5dc6-bcc2-1c89fec829de" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7952 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168155 :tutorial/content "We can find a quadratic form's maximum and minimum values even when it contains cross-product terms. For this, we have the following theorem:\n\n> *Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf x)\\mathbin{:}$*\n> \n> \n> \n> \n> \n> - *The maximum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A{:}$* \n> \n> $$\n> \\max \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}\n> $$\n> \n> \n> \n> \n> \n> \n> *The maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{max}}.$ *\n> \n> - *The minimum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A{:}$* \n> \n> $$\n> \\min \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{min}}\n> $$\n> \n> \n> \n> \n> \n> \n> *The minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{min}}.$ *\n\nSuppose we are given the quadratic form \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})=4x_1^2-2x_1x_2+4x_2^2.\n$$\n\nHow can we find a unit vector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{max}}$ at which the maximum of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is attained?\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +15 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -5)(\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =5,\\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\max \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\: \\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}} = 5.\n$$\n\nTo find $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{max}},$ we need a unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}=5.$ We start by computing $(A-5I)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nFinally, dividing $\\mathbf{v}$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v} \\| = \\sqrt{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "10db6c75-237c-5a28-b5a8-ac25c57cc82f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7953 :tutorial/title "Constrained Optimization of Quadratic Forms"}]}
